================================================================
 IPA v2.6L — power-save: dynamic clock scaling + ipa_rm graph
================================================================

 Incremental patch. Apply ON TOP OF the 6.19 IPA port:

   patch -p1 < patches/sdm660-ipa-port-4.19-to-6.19.nosondy.patch
   patch -p1 < patches/sdm660-ipa-port-6.19-powersave.patch   # <-- this

 WHAT IT DOES
   Re-enables the IPA core-clock dynamic scaling that the base port
   pinned off (ipa_ctx->enable_clock_scaling = 0), and couples it to the
   IPA Resource Manager (ipa_rm) graph.

   Finding vs POSTMARKETOS_X00TD_CELLULAR.md §3: ipa_rm is NOT stubbed —
   all five ipa_rm*.o are compiled (parent Makefile) and the graph is
   wired at runtime (q6_initialize_rm Q6_PROD/CONS, ipa_create_apps_resource
   APPS_CONS, rmnet TX -> WWAN_0_PROD). ipa_rm.c already calls
   ipa2_set_required_perf_profile(); it was inert only because
   enable_clock_scaling was 0 (so the engine always returned NOMINAL).

   So this patch is a single localised change in ipa_v2/ipa.c:
     1. enable_clock_scaling = 1   -> RM votes now move the clock.
     2. Lower the v2.0 bandwidth thresholds (nominal 600->50, turbo
        1000->250 Mbps). The vendor values target gigabit WLAN offload;
        with them every check falls through to SVS=75 MHz even under load
        (the original reason scaling was disabled). HW-measured on X00TD
        (2026-06-08): the aggregate cellular RM vote lands in [150,250)
        Mbps -- higher than the ~100 originally assumed. So nominal=50
        keeps the active vote above NOMINAL, and turbo=250 (above the
        measured vote ceiling) holds it AT NOMINAL instead of letting it
        reach TURBO. Result: idle -> SVS=75, active -> NOMINAL=150,
        TURBO=200 reserved for >250 Mbps (never on cellular). Measured:
        100 MB DL = 2.51 MB/s (~20 Mbps) at NOMINAL, identical to TURBO,
        so the 200->150 MHz drop costs zero throughput.
     3. Boot stays at TURBO (curr_ipa_clk_rate unchanged) for the Q6
        handshake.

 RESULTING BEHAVIOUR (HW-measured, X00TD, 2026-06-08)
   - fully idle:        clock gated off    (active-clients refcount, already)
   - active data:       NOMINAL 150 MHz    (RM vote [150,250) Mbps, turbo=250)
   - active, unvoted:   SVS 75 MHz         (new idle-ish saving; seen within
                                            seconds of the vote releasing)
   - >250 Mbps burst:   TURBO 200 MHz      (unreachable on cellular)
   Cold-start cost: first packet after idle ~43 ms vs ~22 ms warm (one-packet
   penalty); cold HTTPS TTFB 0.27-0.29 s, stable -- no perceptible stutter.

 CAVEATS / TUNING
   - Throughput-proportional scaling needs IPACM/QMI to update the RM perf
     profiles at runtime. With the static 100 Mbps profile the scaling is
     coarse (idle-gated vs active-NOMINAL), not finely load-following.
   - For idle savings to show on the power rail, also drop the board DTS
     "assigned-clock-rates = <200000000>" pin so the driver owns the rate.
   - Hardware-tested on X00TD (2026-06-08): DL unchanged at ~20 Mbps,
     scaling confirmed dynamic (SVS idle <-> NOMINAL active), no stutter.
     If you re-tune: to keep active out of SVS, lower
     clock_scaling_bw_threshold_nominal (raising it enlarges the SVS
     region and makes it worse). turbo=250 deliberately parks active at
     NOMINAL; lower it toward 150 only if you WANT active to ride TURBO
     (200 MHz) -- measured to give no extra throughput here.

 Generated: 2026-06-04
================================================================

--- a/drivers/platform/msm/ipa/ipa_v2/ipa.c
+++ b/drivers/platform/msm/ipa/ipa_v2/ipa.c
@@ -3974,19 +3974,47 @@ static int ipa_init(const struct ipa_plat_drv_res *resource_p,
 	if (result)
 		goto fail_clk;
 
-	/* Mainline tuning: disable dynamic clock scaling.
+	/* Power-save: re-enable dynamic IPA core-clock scaling and let the
+	 * IPA Resource Manager graph drive it.
 	 *
-	 * Vendor enables scaling which picks IPA core_clk rate (75/150/200 MHz)
-	 * from current bandwidth_mbps. At our LTE baseline ~0.5 Mbps, every
-	 * threshold check falls through to SVS = 75 MHz — the slowest setting,
-	 * which halves processing capacity for no reason.
+	 * The ipa_rm* objects are compiled in and already wired:
+	 *   - q6_initialize_rm()        creates Q6_PROD / Q6_CONS (perf 100 Mbps)
+	 *   - ipa_create_apps_resource() creates APPS_CONS
+	 *   - rmnet TX path requests WWAN_0_PROD via the inactivity timer
+	 * ipa_rm aggregates the per-resource perf profiles and calls
+	 * ipa2_set_required_perf_profile() (see ipa_rm.c). That call only moves
+	 * the clock when enable_clock_scaling is set; with it cleared the engine
+	 * pinned NOMINAL (150 MHz) and the RM votes were inert. Re-enabling it
+	 * couples the RM graph to the core clock: gated off when idle (active-
+	 * clients refcount), SVS (75 MHz) when active-but-unvoted, NOMINAL/TURBO
+	 * under the RM bandwidth vote.
 	 *
-	 * With scaling disabled, ipa2_set_clock_plan_from_pm() lands in the
-	 * else-branch that pins needed_voltage = IPA_VOLTAGE_NOMINAL (150 MHz).
-	 * curr_ipa_clk_rate is still initialised to TURBO here, so init paths
-	 * see full bandwidth before NOMINAL takes over.
+	 * Threshold retune. The vendor v2.0 thresholds (nominal 600 / turbo
+	 * 1000 Mbps) target gigabit WLAN-offload, so every check fell through to
+	 * SVS = 75 MHz even under load — exactly why scaling was disabled.
+	 * HW-measured on X00TD (2026-06-08): the aggregate cellular RM vote sits
+	 * in [150,250) Mbps, higher than the ~100 first assumed. So nominal=50
+	 * keeps the active vote clear of SVS, and turbo=250 (above the measured
+	 * vote ceiling) parks active AT NOMINAL (150 MHz) rather than TURBO. A
+	 * 100 MB DL ran at 2.51 MB/s (~20 Mbps) at NOMINAL — identical to TURBO,
+	 * so the 200->150 MHz drop is free. SVS stays for idle, TURBO (200) is
+	 * reserved for >250 Mbps and never reached on cellular. Tune: lower
+	 * nominal to keep active out of SVS; lower turbo toward 150 only to let
+	 * active ride TURBO (no measured throughput gain here).
+	 *
+	 * curr_ipa_clk_rate stays initialised to TURBO so the boot / Q6 handshake
+	 * runs at full clock before the RM-driven profile takes over.
+	 *
+	 * NOTE: throughput-proportional scaling needs IPACM/QMI to update the RM
+	 * perf profiles at runtime; with the static profile the scaling is coarse
+	 * (binary: SVS idle vs NOMINAL active, not load-following). For idle
+	 * savings to show on the rail, also drop the board DTS
+	 * "assigned-clock-rates = <200000000>" pin so the driver — not RPM —
+	 * owns the rate.
 	 */
-	ipa_ctx->enable_clock_scaling = 0;
+	ipa_ctx->enable_clock_scaling = 1;
+	ipa_ctx->ctrl->clock_scaling_bw_threshold_nominal = 50;
+	ipa_ctx->ctrl->clock_scaling_bw_threshold_turbo = 250;
 	ipa_ctx->curr_ipa_clk_rate = ipa_ctx->ctrl->ipa_clk_rate_turbo;
 
 	/* enable IPA clocks explicitly to allow the initialization */
